/usr/bin/copy from the Micronix 1.6 distribution

There is no source for copy, so this is the disassembly, and copy.c is that
disassembly read back into C.

    disas -f copy.ctl copy.dist

copy.dist is the binary off inode 58 of disks/dist/1012-8_dist_2.IMD, 3486
bytes, mode 0711, dated 7 August 1982.  It has no header - see
../newuser/README - and uses Whitesmith's runtime, c.ent at 0dde and c.ents at
0c91.  It is the program of that name in Kernighan and Plauger's Software
Tools.

Its own code is main at 010e and nothing else: it attaches the standard output
with the library's finit, and then calls getchar at 02bf and putchar at 0339
until the character is the -1 of the end of the file.  Everything from 0159
on is the library's stdio, malloc and the system call stubs.  Every byte is
copied as it is, and the manual's note - that on some systems a binary file
would be mangled and cp should be used, "but not Micronix" - is the reason it
is a program at all.

The exit status was the one thing wrong with it: main falls out through
c.rets with the -1 still in BC, the startup passes it to exit, and so every
run exits 255.  It exits 0 here.

Checked in usersim against the original, byte for byte, on text, on a file
with every one of the 256 byte values twenty times over, and on four thousand
random bytes.
